{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 2970,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "WHAT IS THE SHAPE OF SPACE?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3970,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re used to thinking of space as the emptiness\nin which things happen, like an empty warehouse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7710,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ready to be filled, or a theater stage on\nwhich the events of the Universe play out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12220,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But General Relativity predicts that space\nis not just emptiness, it’s a physical,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16820,
    "dDurationMs": 5109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dynamic thing, and that prediction has been\nborne out by many, many experiments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21929,
    "dDurationMs": 4231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space can bend because of matter and energy,\ncurving the paths of objects that move inside"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26160,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27160,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can ripple with gravitational waves And\nit can expand, creating more and more space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31570,
    "dDurationMs": 1829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between two objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33399,
    "dDurationMs": 7291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of these phenomena can be described by\none idea: curvature of space (or spacetime)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40690,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In flat regions of spacetime (like, if there’s\nno energy or matter nearby), objects traveling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45430,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "along parallel paths stay along parallel paths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48650,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In positively curved regions of spacetime\n(like near planets or black holes), parallel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52820,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paths converge, and in negatively curved regions\nof spacetime parallel paths (or even paths"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58410,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pointed at each other!) diverge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60600,
    "dDurationMs": 4519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about space as a whole ? If space\nis positively curved everywhere, then there’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65119,
    "dDurationMs": 4371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only one shape space can be: a giant hyper-potato."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69490,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you went in one direction for long enough,\neventually you’d end up in the same place"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73070,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you started."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74070,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If space is flat everywhere, its shape could\nbe simple: just extend out straight to infinity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78660,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or it could loop around in a periodic way,\nlike in some video games:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82580,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if space is negatively curved everywhere,\nsports would be impossible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86960,
    "dDurationMs": 1030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So which is it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87990,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are basically two ways to measure the\nlarge-scale curvature of the Universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91860,
    "dDurationMs": 2130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One is to measure the angles inside of triangles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93990,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the space is flat, then the angles will\nadd up to 180 degrees."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98060,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if the space is curved, those angles will\nadd up to more or less than 180 degrees depending"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102920,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the type of curvature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104790,
    "dDurationMs": 3490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cosmologists have done the equivalent of measuring\nour Universe’s triangles by looking at a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108280,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "picture of the early Universe, and studying\nthe spatial relationship between different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112080,
    "dDurationMs": 1329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "points on that picture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113409,
    "dDurationMs": 3621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second way to measure curvature is to\nmeasure the thing that causes space to curve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117030,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the first place: the density of energy\nand matter throughout the Universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121100,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which cosmologists have also measured."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123350,
    "dDurationMs": 4659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that in both cases, measurements\nshow the Universe to be… pretty much flat"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128009,
    "dDurationMs": 2191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(within 0.4% margin of error)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130200,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But before you get disappointed that we don’t\nlive in a cool cosmic hyper-potato, let me"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134200,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tell you one big problem"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135730,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fact that we live in a flat Universe appears\nto be a GIGANTIC, COSMIC-LEVEL COINCIDENCE."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141100,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the Universe had just a little bit more\nmass and energy, space would have curved one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145870,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146870,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if it had just a little bit less mass\nand energy, space would have curved the other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150420,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151420,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we seem to have just the right amount\nto make space perfectly flat as far as we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155060,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can tell."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156060,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This perfect amount is the equivalent of five\nhydrogen atoms per cubic meter of space, on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161610,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "average."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166380,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If instead there were six hydrogen atoms per\ncubic meter of space on average, or four,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171350,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entire Universe would have been a lot\nmore curved or a lot less ."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175450,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we so far have no idea why our universe\nhas the density that it does."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180560,
    "dDurationMs": 35660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When it comes to the curvature of the universe,\nour knowledge falls flat."
    } ]
  } ]
}
